// The flashcards: [id, front, back, more]. `more` is optional detail shown under the answer.
// Ids are permanent (progress is keyed by them): never renumber; add new cards at the end
// of a topic with the next free number. The first four letters are the topic id.
// Text may use <sub>, <sup>, <b>, <i> and <br>.
export const CARDS = [
  // ───── Radiation physics ─────
  ['phys01', 'Name the three main photon interactions in tissue and how each depends on Z.',
    'Photoelectric: τ/ρ ∝ Z³/E³<br>Compton: σ/ρ ∝ electrons per gram, nearly independent of Z<br>Pair production: κ/ρ ∝ Z (κ per atom ∝ Z²)',
    'Per atom, photoelectric scales as about Z⁴; per gram it is about Z³. Hydrogen has about twice the electrons per gram of other elements, so hydrogen-rich tissue has slightly higher Compton attenuation per gram.'],
  ['phys02', 'What is the threshold energy for pair production? For triplet production?',
    'Pair production: 1.022 MeV (2m₀c²), in the field of a nucleus.<br>Triplet production: 2.044 MeV (4m₀c²), in the field of an electron.',
    'Energy above the threshold is shared as kinetic energy by the electron and positron. The positron later annihilates, giving two 511 keV photons.'],
  ['phys03', 'Over what energy range does Compton scattering dominate in water and soft tissue?',
    'About 30 keV to about 25 MeV.',
    'Below ~30 keV the photoelectric effect dominates; above ~25 MeV pair production does. That is why megavoltage therapy beams give almost no bone/soft-tissue contrast.'],
  ['phys04', 'Write the Compton formula for the energy of the scattered photon.',
    'hν′ = hν / [1 + α(1 − cos θ)], where α = hν/m₀c² and m₀c² = 0.511 MeV.',
    'The wavelength shift is Δλ = 0.0243 Å × (1 − cos θ), independent of the incident energy.'],
  ['phys05', 'For a high-energy photon, what is the maximum energy of a Compton photon scattered at 90°? At 180°?',
    '90°: 0.511 MeV.<br>180° (backscatter): 0.255 MeV.',
    'These limits are what make scattered radiation in shielding much softer than the primary beam, whatever the primary energy.'],
  ['phys06', 'Describe the photoelectric effect and what follows it.',
    'The photon is absorbed by a tightly bound (usually K-shell) electron, which is ejected with kinetic energy hν − E<sub>B</sub>. The vacancy is filled, giving characteristic x-rays or Auger electrons.',
    'It is most likely when hν is just above a shell’s binding energy, which gives the sharp absorption edges. Fluorescence yield (x-rays rather than Auger electrons) rises with Z.'],
  ['phys07', 'Give the K-edge energies of iodine, barium, gadolinium, tungsten and lead.',
    'I 33.2 keV · Ba 37.4 keV · Gd 50.2 keV · W 69.5 keV · Pb 88.0 keV',
    'Contrast agents are bright when the spectrum sits just above their K-edge, which is why iodine contrast works best around 60–80 kVp.'],
  ['phys08', 'How are the linear attenuation coefficient, HVL and mean free path related?',
    'HVL = ln 2 / μ = 0.693/μ<br>Mean free path = 1/μ = 1.44 × HVL',
    'TVL = ln 10 / μ = 2.303/μ = 3.32 HVL. These hold for a narrow, monoenergetic beam.'],
  ['phys09', 'What is beam hardening, and how does it show up in HVL measurements?',
    'Low-energy photons are removed preferentially, so the mean energy of a polyenergetic beam rises with depth. The second HVL is larger than the first.',
    'Homogeneity coefficient = HVL₁/HVL₂, which is below 1 for a polyenergetic beam and equal to 1 for a monoenergetic one. In CT, beam hardening causes cupping and dark streaks between dense objects.'],
  ['phys10', 'How do collisional and radiative stopping powers depend on Z and electron energy? What is the critical energy?',
    'Radiative (bremsstrahlung) losses per gram scale roughly as Z × E; collisional losses per gram fall slowly with Z. They are equal at the critical energy, roughly 800/Z MeV.',
    'About 10 MeV in lead and around 90 MeV in water. That is why high-Z targets are used for bremsstrahlung and low-Z materials (like Lucite) for beta shielding.'],
  ['phys11', 'About how efficient is an x-ray tube at producing x-rays?',
    'Efficiency ≈ 9 × 10⁻¹⁰ × Z × V (V in volts): about 0.7% for tungsten at 100 kV. The rest becomes heat.',
    'Efficiency rises with tube voltage, which is why megavoltage targets produce much more bremsstrahlung per electron, mostly in the forward direction.'],
  ['phys12', 'How does the stopping power of a heavy charged particle depend on its charge and speed?',
    'S ∝ z²/v² (Bethe–Bloch). Energy loss rises as the particle slows, producing the Bragg peak near the end of its range.',
    'The dose depends on the particle’s charge squared, not its mass. An alpha (z = 2) loses about four times as much energy per unit length as a proton at the same speed.'],
  ['phys13', 'Give the rules of thumb for electron ranges in water.',
    'Practical range R<sub>p</sub> ≈ E/2 cm<br>R80 ≈ E/2.8 cm<br>R90 ≈ E/3.2 cm<br>(E in MeV)',
    'So a 12 MeV beam treats to about 3.75 cm (90%) and is essentially gone by 6 cm.'],
  ['phys14', 'State the decay law and the relations between λ, T½ and mean life.',
    'A = A₀e<sup>−λt</sup><br>λ = 0.693/T½<br>Mean life τ = 1/λ = 1.44 T½',
    'The total number of decays of a source that decays completely is A₀τ = 1.44 A₀T½, which is the basis of permanent-implant and MIRD cumulated-activity calculations.'],
  ['phys15', 'Contrast transient and secular equilibrium, with an example of each.',
    'Transient: the parent lives longer than the daughter, but not by much (Mo-99 → Tc-99m). The daughter ends up decaying with the parent’s half-life, at a slightly higher activity.<br>Secular: the parent lives far longer (Ra-226 → Rn-222, Sr-90 → Y-90). Daughter activity equals parent activity.',
    'In transient equilibrium, A<sub>d</sub> = A<sub>p</sub> × T<sub>p</sub>/(T<sub>p</sub> − T<sub>d</sub>) × branching fraction. For Mo-99, 87.5% of decays go to Tc-99m. If the daughter lives longer than the parent, there is no equilibrium.'],
  ['phys16', 'When is Tc-99m activity in a generator at its maximum after elution?',
    'About 23 hours.',
    't<sub>max</sub> = ln(λ<sub>d</sub>/λ<sub>p</sub>) / (λ<sub>d</sub> − λ<sub>p</sub>). With T½ 66 h for Mo-99 and 6.0 h for Tc-99m, this gives about 23 h, which is why generators are eluted once a day.'],
  ['phys17', 'When can a nucleus decay by positron emission rather than only by electron capture?',
    'When the parent–daughter atomic mass difference exceeds 1.022 MeV. Below that, only electron capture is possible.',
    'Electron capture leaves an inner-shell vacancy, so it is always followed by characteristic x-rays or Auger electrons. Tl-201 and I-123 decay by electron capture.'],
  ['phys18', 'What are isomeric transition and internal conversion?',
    'Isomeric transition: a metastable excited nucleus (like Tc-99m) drops to its ground state, emitting a gamma ray.<br>Internal conversion: instead of a gamma, the nucleus gives the energy directly to an orbital electron, which is ejected.',
    'Internal conversion is followed by characteristic x-rays or Auger electrons, and adds non-penetrating dose. Tc-99m’s 140 keV transition is about 89% gamma emission.'],
  ['phys19', 'Convert between curies and becquerels.',
    '1 Ci = 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq<br>1 mCi = 37 MBq<br>1 MBq = 27 µCi',
    'The curie was originally the activity of 1 g of radium-226.'],
  ['phys20', 'How are a photon’s energy and wavelength related, in handy units?',
    'E (keV) = 12.4 / λ (Å)',
    'From E = hc/λ, with hc = 12.4 keV·Å = 1.24 keV·nm.'],
  ['phys21', 'Define the roentgen and give W/e for air.',
    '1 R = 2.58 × 10⁻⁴ C/kg of air.<br>W<sub>air</sub>/e = 33.97 J/C (33.97 eV per ion pair).',
    'So 1 R in air corresponds to an air kerma of about 8.76 mGy (0.876 cGy/R).'],
  ['phys22', 'What is Rayleigh (coherent) scattering, and when does it matter?',
    'The photon scatters off the atom as a whole, with no energy transferred and no ionization, mostly at small angles.',
    'It matters only at low energies, contributing a few percent of attenuation in the diagnostic range and essentially none at megavoltage energies.'],
  ['phys23', 'Why is hydrogen the best neutron moderator?',
    'A neutron loses the most energy in an elastic collision with a nucleus of the same mass: on average half its energy per collision with a proton.',
    'Hence hydrogenous materials (water, polyethylene, concrete) moderate neutrons. Thermal neutrons are then captured, e.g. by ¹⁰B (borated polyethylene) or ¹H (which emits a 2.2 MeV gamma).'],
  ['phys24', 'At what beam energies do linacs produce photoneutrons, and why does it matter?',
    'Above about 10 MV, through (γ,n) reactions in the target, flattening filter, collimators and room, with thresholds of roughly 7–10 MeV for most materials.',
    'Neutrons need extra shielding at the maze door (often borated polyethylene and lead) and activate linac components, leaving short-lived induced activity after high-energy beams.'],
  ['phys25', 'How do μ/ρ, μ<sub>tr</sub>/ρ and μ<sub>en</sub>/ρ differ?',
    'μ/ρ: probability of any interaction.<br>μ<sub>tr</sub>/ρ: the fraction of photon energy transferred to charged particles.<br>μ<sub>en</sub>/ρ = μ<sub>tr</sub>/ρ × (1 − g): the part not lost again as bremsstrahlung.',
    'Collision kerma uses μ<sub>en</sub>/ρ: K<sub>col</sub> = Ψ × μ<sub>en</sub>/ρ, with Ψ the energy fluence.'],
  ['phys26', 'Give the main characteristic x-ray energies of tungsten, molybdenum and rhodium.',
    'W: Kα ≈ 59 keV, Kβ ≈ 67 keV<br>Mo: Kα 17.5 keV, Kβ 19.6 keV<br>Rh: Kα 20.2 keV, Kβ 22.7 keV',
    'Molybdenum and rhodium targets are used in mammography because their characteristic lines fall in the 17–23 keV range best for breast contrast. W lines appear only above its 69.5 keV K-edge.'],
  ['phys27', 'State the inverse square law.',
    'I₁/I₂ = (d₂/d₁)²: doubling the distance cuts the intensity to a quarter.',
    'It applies to a point source with no attenuation or scatter. In practice, distances are measured from the effective source position.'],
  ['phys28', 'What happens when a positron annihilates?',
    'It combines with an electron, usually after coming nearly to rest, emitting two 511 keV photons about 180° apart.',
    'The slight residual momentum makes them not quite collinear (about ±0.25°), which blurs PET resolution, more so for large rings.'],

  // ───── Dosimetry ─────
  ['dosi01', 'Define kerma and its two components.',
    'Kerma K = dE<sub>tr</sub>/dm, the kinetic energy given to charged particles per unit mass (Gy).<br>K = K<sub>col</sub> + K<sub>rad</sub>.',
    'K<sub>col</sub> = Ψ × (μ<sub>en</sub>/ρ) is the part spent locally in collisions; K<sub>rad</sub> is the part re-emitted as bremsstrahlung.'],
  ['dosi02', 'When does absorbed dose equal collision kerma?',
    'Under charged-particle equilibrium (CPE).',
    'In a megavoltage beam beyond d<sub>max</sub> there is only transient CPE: D = βK<sub>col</sub>, with β slightly greater than 1 because the electrons were set in motion upstream where the photon fluence was higher. In the buildup region, D < K<sub>col</sub>.'],
  ['dosi03', 'What is the radiative fraction g?',
    'The fraction of the energy given to charged particles that is lost again as bremsstrahlung: K<sub>col</sub> = K(1 − g).',
    'For Co-60 in air, g ≈ 0.003. It increases with electron energy and with Z.'],
  ['dosi04', 'State the Bragg–Gray relation and its conditions.',
    'D<sub>med</sub> = D<sub>gas</sub> × (S̄/ρ)<sup>med</sup><sub>gas</sub><br>Conditions: the cavity is small compared with the electrons’ ranges, so it doesn’t disturb the electron fluence; and the dose in it comes only from electrons crossing it (no photon interactions inside it).',
    'For megavoltage photons, (S/ρ)<sup>water</sup><sub>air</sub> ≈ 1.10–1.13, falling slowly with energy.'],
  ['dosi05', 'How does Spencer–Attix cavity theory improve on Bragg–Gray?',
    'It includes secondary electrons (delta rays) by using restricted stopping powers with a cutoff energy Δ (about 10 keV for typical chambers), plus a track-end term.',
    'Δ is the energy of an electron just able to cross the cavity. These ratios are the ones used in TG-51 and TRS-398 k<sub>Q</sub> values.'],
  ['dosi06', 'What governs dose in a large photon-detector cavity? What about in between?',
    'Large cavity (CPE inside it): D<sub>med</sub> = D<sub>det</sub> × (μ<sub>en</sub>/ρ)<sup>med</sup><sub>det</sub>.<br>In between: Burlin cavity theory, a weighted mix of the stopping-power and μ<sub>en</sub>/ρ ratios.',
    'TLD chips and diodes in kilovoltage beams are often closer to large cavities; ion chambers in MV beams are Bragg–Gray cavities.'],
  ['dosi07', 'Write the TG-51 dose equation for photon beams.',
    'D<sub>w</sub><sup>Q</sup> = M × k<sub>Q</sub> × N<sub>D,w</sub><sup>60Co</sup><br>M = P<sub>ion</sub> P<sub>TP</sub> P<sub>elec</sub> P<sub>pol</sub> M<sub>raw</sub>',
    'N<sub>D,w</sub><sup>60Co</sup> is the chamber’s absorbed-dose-to-water calibration from an ADCL; k<sub>Q</sub> converts it to the beam quality Q. Calibrations should be no more than two years old.'],
  ['dosi08', 'Give the TG-51 temperature–pressure correction.',
    'P<sub>TP</sub> = [(273.2 + T)/295.2] × [101.33/P]<br>T in °C, P in kPa',
    'Reference conditions are 22 °C and 101.33 kPa (1 atm). Warmer air or lower pressure means less air mass in a vented chamber, so P<sub>TP</sub> > 1. Let the chamber reach the water temperature before measuring.'],
  ['dosi09', 'What is the photon beam-quality specifier in TG-51? In TRS-398?',
    'TG-51: %dd(10)<sub>x</sub>, the photon-only PDD at 10 cm depth for a 10 × 10 cm² field at 100 cm SSD. For 10 MV and up, a 1 mm lead foil removes contaminant electrons during measurement.<br>TRS-398: TPR<sub>20,10</sub>.',
    'TG-51 reference depth for photons is 10 cm, at either 100 cm SSD or SAD set-up.'],
  ['dosi10', 'What are the electron beam-quality specifier and reference depth in TG-51?',
    'Quality: R<sub>50</sub>, the depth of 50% dose.<br>d<sub>ref</sub> = 0.6 R<sub>50</sub> − 0.1 cm',
    'R<sub>50</sub> is found from I<sub>50</sub> (the depth of 50% ionization): R<sub>50</sub> = 1.029 I<sub>50</sub> − 0.06 cm for I<sub>50</sub> ≤ 10 cm. Plane-parallel chambers are recommended for R<sub>50</sub> ≤ 2.6 cm (≲ 6 MeV).'],
  ['dosi11', 'How is P<sub>ion</sub> measured in a pulsed beam?',
    'Two-voltage technique: P<sub>ion</sub> = (1 − V<sub>H</sub>/V<sub>L</sub>) / (M<sub>H</sub>/M<sub>L</sub> − V<sub>H</sub>/V<sub>L</sub>), with V<sub>H</sub>/V<sub>L</sub> usually 2.',
    'It corrects for ions lost to recombination. TG-51 says not to use a chamber whose P<sub>ion</sub> exceeds 1.05. Recombination grows with dose per pulse, which is high in FFF beams.'],
  ['dosi12', 'How is the polarity correction measured?',
    'P<sub>pol</sub> = |(M<sub>raw</sub><sup>+</sup> − M<sub>raw</sub><sup>−</sup>) / (2M<sub>raw</sub>)|, where M<sub>raw</sub> is the reading at the polarity normally used.',
    'Polarity effects are larger in electron beams and for plane-parallel chambers.'],
  ['dosi13', 'Give typical depths of maximum dose for Co-60, 6, 10 and 18 MV beams.',
    'Co-60: 0.5 cm · 6 MV: 1.5 cm · 10 MV: 2.5 cm · 18 MV: about 3.3 cm',
    'd<sub>max</sub> shrinks slightly with larger fields because of contaminant electrons from the head.'],
  ['dosi14', 'How does photon PDD change with energy, field size, SSD and depth?',
    'PDD increases with energy (beyond d<sub>max</sub>), with field size (more scatter) and with SSD (gentler inverse square falloff). It decreases with depth.',
    'The SSD dependence is captured by the Mayneord F factor.'],
  ['dosi15', 'Write the Mayneord F factor.',
    'F = [(f₂ + d<sub>m</sub>)/(f₁ + d<sub>m</sub>)]² × [(f₁ + d)/(f₂ + d)]²<br>PDD(f₂) ≈ PDD(f₁) × F',
    'It corrects only the inverse square part, not the change in scatter, so it slightly overestimates for large fields and deep points.'],
  ['dosi16', 'Define TAR, TPR and TMR. Which is independent of SSD?',
    'TAR: dose at depth ÷ dose in free space at the same point.<br>TPR: dose at depth ÷ dose at a reference depth at the same point.<br>TMR: a TPR whose reference depth is d<sub>max</sub>.<br>TPR and TMR are essentially independent of SSD.',
    'They are used for isocentric (SAD) calculations; PDD is used for SSD set-ups.'],
  ['dosi17', 'What are S<sub>c</sub> and S<sub>p</sub>, and how are they measured?',
    'S<sub>c</sub> (collimator or head scatter): the change in output with collimator setting, measured in air with a buildup cap or miniphantom.<br>S<sub>p</sub> (phantom scatter): the change with field size irradiated in the patient.<br>S<sub>c,p</sub> = S<sub>c</sub> × S<sub>p</sub> is measured in phantom.',
    'With blocks or MLC, S<sub>c</sub> uses the collimator (jaw) setting, and S<sub>p</sub> uses the blocked field size.'],
  ['dosi18', 'What is the equivalent square of a rectangular field?',
    'Side = 4A/P = 2ab/(a + b)',
    'For example, a 5 × 20 cm field is about an 8 × 8 cm square. An equivalent circle has radius ≈ 0.56 × side.'],
  ['dosi19', 'Write the monitor unit equation for an isocentric (SAD) set-up.',
    'MU = D / [Ḋ<sub>ref</sub> × S<sub>c</sub>(c) × S<sub>p</sub>(s) × TMR(d, s) × WF × TF × ISF]',
    'Ḋ<sub>ref</sub> is the calibrated output (often 1 cGy/MU at d<sub>max</sub>, 10 × 10); ISF is the inverse square factor if the calibration point is not at the point of calculation; WF and TF are wedge and tray factors.'],
  ['dosi20', 'Compare TLDs and OSLDs.',
    'TLD (LiF:Mg,Ti): read by heating to give a glow curve; Z<sub>eff</sub> 8.2, close to tissue; reading erases the signal; annealed for reuse.<br>OSLD (Al₂O₃:C): read with light; can be re-read; Z<sub>eff</sub> ≈ 11, so it over-responds at low energies.',
    'Both are passive integrating dosimeters used for in vivo checks, mailed audits and personnel monitoring.'],
  ['dosi21', 'What are the advantages and dependences of diode detectors?',
    'Advantages: high sensitivity, small size, instant readout.<br>Dependences: temperature, dose per pulse, energy, beam angle, and accumulated dose (radiation damage).',
    'Used for in vivo dosimetry, scanning small fields and arrays. Unshielded diodes over-respond to low-energy scatter.'],
  ['dosi22', 'Why is radiochromic film preferred over radiographic film for dosimetry?',
    'It self-develops (no processor), is nearly tissue-equivalent with little energy dependence, has very high spatial resolution, and is insensitive to room light.',
    'Read on a flatbed scanner, usually in the red channel (or several channels). Radiographic (silver halide) film over-responds strongly at low energy because of its high-Z silver.'],
  ['dosi23', 'What makes small-field dosimetry hard, and what report covers it?',
    'Loss of lateral charged-particle equilibrium, partial occlusion of the source by the collimator, and detector volume averaging and perturbation.',
    'IAEA TRS-483 (with AAPM) gives the formalism and field output correction factors k<sup>f<sub>clin</sub>,f<sub>msr</sub></sup><sub>Q<sub>clin</sub>,Q<sub>msr</sub></sub>. Use small detectors and cross-check with more than one type.'],
  ['dosi24', 'How do you estimate the mean electron energy at the surface and at depth?',
    'Surface: Ē₀ = 2.33 × R<sub>50</sub> (MeV, with R<sub>50</sub> in cm).<br>At depth z: Ē<sub>z</sub> = Ē₀(1 − z/R<sub>p</sub>) (Harder).',
    'Ē<sub>z</sub> is needed to choose stopping-power ratios at depth.'],
  ['dosi25', 'How do electron depth-dose curves differ from photon ones?',
    'High surface dose (about 75–95%, rising with energy), a broad shoulder, then a steep falloff and a low bremsstrahlung tail of a few percent that rises with energy.',
    'Unlike photons, the electron surface dose increases with energy.'],
  ['dosi26', 'How much does 1 Gy heat water? What dosimeter relies on this?',
    'About 0.24 mK (water’s specific heat is about 4186 J/kg·K).',
    'Calorimeters measure absorbed dose absolutely, and are the basis of primary standards for absorbed dose to water.'],
  ['dosi27', 'What is the Fricke dosimeter, and what is its G-value?',
    'A ferrous sulfate solution in which radiation oxidizes Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺, read by spectrophotometry. G(Fe³⁺) ≈ 15.5 ions per 100 eV (1.61 µmol/J) for Co-60.',
    'A chemical dosimeter, accurate and near water-equivalent, but needing large doses (tens of Gy).'],
  ['dosi28', 'How is an ion chamber calibration traced to a national standard?',
    'Through an Accredited Dosimetry Calibration Laboratory (ADCL), whose standards trace to NIST.',
    'TG-51 asks for chamber calibration at least every two years, and for electrometers too, separately or together.'],
  ['dosi29', 'What are wedge and tray factors?',
    'The ratio of dose with the wedge (or tray) in the beam to the dose without it, at a reference point on the central axis.',
    'Physical wedge factors vary with field size and depth because the wedge hardens the beam. Enhanced (dynamic) wedges vary with field size but have no hardening.'],

  // ───── Radiobiology ─────
  ['rbio01', 'Write the linear-quadratic survival equation. What does α/β mean?',
    'S = e<sup>−αD − βD²</sup><br>α/β is the dose at which the linear (αD) and quadratic (βD²) components kill equally.',
    'The α term represents single-hit lethal events; the β term represents two separate sub-lethal events interacting, which can be repaired if they are far apart in time.'],
  ['rbio02', 'Write the biologically effective dose (BED) for n fractions of d.',
    'BED = nd × [1 + d/(α/β)]',
    'BED is expressed in Gy with the α/β as a subscript (for example 72 Gy₁₀). Add a repopulation term (−(T − T<sub>k</sub>) ln 2 / (αT<sub>pot</sub>)) for long treatments.'],
  ['rbio03', 'How do you convert a schedule to its equivalent dose in 2 Gy fractions (EQD2)?',
    'EQD2 = nd × (d + α/β) / (2 + α/β) = BED / (1 + 2/(α/β))',
    'EQD2 allows comparison with conventional constraints. For a 2 Gy-per-fraction schedule, EQD2 = the total dose.'],
  ['rbio04', 'Give typical α/β values for tumors, early- and late-responding tissue, and a few low-α/β tumors.',
    'Tumors and early-responding tissue: ≈ 10 Gy<br>Late-responding tissue: ≈ 3 Gy<br>Prostate cancer: ≈ 1.5 Gy; breast: ≈ 3–4 Gy; melanoma and some sarcomas are also low.',
    'Low α/β means more sensitivity to fraction size, which is the rationale for hypofractionation in prostate and breast.'],
  ['rbio05', 'What are the 5 Rs of radiobiology?',
    'Repair (of sublethal damage), Redistribution (reassortment through the cell cycle), Repopulation, Reoxygenation, and intrinsic Radiosensitivity.',
    'Fractionation spares normal tissue mainly by repair, while redistribution and reoxygenation tend to make tumors more sensitive between fractions. Repopulation works against long treatment times.'],
  ['rbio06', 'Which phases of the cell cycle are most and least radiosensitive?',
    'Most sensitive: M (mitosis) and G2.<br>Most resistant: late S.',
    'Cells with a long G1 also show a resistant period early in G1. Homologous recombination, available in S and G2, explains the resistance of late S.'],
  ['rbio07', 'Define the oxygen enhancement ratio and give typical values.',
    'OER = dose under hypoxia ÷ dose with oxygen for the same effect.<br>≈ 2.5–3 for x-rays (lower, around 2, at low doses); ≈ 1 for high-LET alphas.',
    'Oxygen “fixes” free-radical damage to DNA so it cannot be chemically restored. It must be present during irradiation, and its effect saturates by about 20 mmHg.'],
  ['rbio08', 'How does RBE vary with LET?',
    'RBE rises with LET to a maximum near 100 keV/µm, then falls.',
    'At about 100 keV/µm the spacing of ionizations matches the diameter of the DNA double helix (≈ 2 nm), making double-strand breaks most likely. Above it, energy is wasted (overkill). OER falls as LET rises.'],
  ['rbio09', 'Compare direct and indirect action.',
    'Direct: the radiation ionizes the DNA itself.<br>Indirect: it ionizes water, producing free radicals (chiefly OH•) that damage the DNA.',
    'About two-thirds of x-ray damage in mammalian cells is indirect, which is why free-radical scavengers can protect. High-LET radiation acts mainly directly.'],
  ['rbio10', 'Roughly how many DNA lesions does 1 Gy cause per cell? Which one kills?',
    'About 1000 single-strand breaks and about 20–40 double-strand breaks. Double-strand breaks are the critical lethal lesion.',
    'Lethal chromosome aberrations include dicentrics, rings and anaphase bridges; stable ones such as translocations are not lethal but can be carcinogenic. Dicentrics are used for biological dosimetry.'],
  ['rbio11', 'What are the two main pathways for repairing double-strand breaks?',
    'Non-homologous end joining (NHEJ): works throughout the cycle, mainly G1; fast but error-prone.<br>Homologous recombination (HR): S and G2, using the sister chromatid as a template; accurate.',
    'Defects in these pathways (for example ATM in ataxia-telangiectasia) cause extreme radiosensitivity.'],
  ['rbio12', 'Contrast deterministic and stochastic effects.',
    'Deterministic (tissue reactions): a threshold dose; severity increases with dose (erythema, cataract, sterility).<br>Stochastic: no threshold assumed; probability increases with dose, severity does not (cancer, heritable effects).',
    'Radiation protection limits aim to prevent deterministic effects and keep stochastic risk acceptably low, assuming a linear no-threshold (LNT) model.'],
  ['rbio13', 'What are the ICRP 103 nominal risk coefficients for stochastic effects?',
    'Cancer: 5.5%/Sv (whole population), 4.1%/Sv (adult workers).<br>Heritable effects: 0.2%/Sv (whole population), 0.1%/Sv (workers).<br>Total ≈ 5.7%/Sv and 4.2%/Sv.',
    'The rounded “5% per sievert” is used for quick estimates. ICRP 60 had put heritable risk higher, at 1.3%/Sv.'],
  ['rbio14', 'Give the three acute radiation syndromes and their rough dose ranges.',
    'Hematopoietic: from about 1 Gy, with LD<sub>50/60</sub> ≈ 3–4 Gy without treatment.<br>Gastrointestinal: above about 6–10 Gy.<br>Cerebrovascular (neurovascular): above about 20–50 Gy; death in days.',
    'With supportive care (antibiotics, transfusions, growth factors), the LD<sub>50/60</sub> rises to about 6–7 Gy. Prodromal nausea and vomiting within hours suggests a dose above about 1–2 Gy.'],
  ['rbio15', 'What is the ICRP threshold for radiation cataract, and the lens dose limit?',
    'Threshold: about 0.5 Gy (ICRP 118, 2011).<br>ICRP occupational lens limit: 20 mSv/yr averaged over 5 years (no single year above 50 mSv). The NRC limit is still 150 mSv/yr.',
    'Radiation cataracts typically start at the posterior subcapsular region, which helps distinguish them from age-related ones.'],
  ['rbio16', 'Give the approximate skin-injury dose bands for fluoroscopically guided procedures.',
    '&lt; 2 Gy: no visible effect expected<br>2–5 Gy: transient erythema, temporary epilation<br>5–10 Gy: erythema, prolonged epilation, possible dry desquamation<br>10–15 Gy: moist desquamation, permanent epilation<br>&gt; 15 Gy: ulceration and necrosis likely',
    'Bands follow Balter et al. (2010). A peak skin dose above 15 Gy is a Joint Commission sentinel event. Effects may not appear for weeks.'],
  ['rbio17', 'Describe the effects of radiation on the embryo and fetus by stage.',
    '0–2 weeks (preimplantation): all-or-none; death or normal development.<br>2–8 weeks (organogenesis): malformations (threshold ≈ 100–200 mGy).<br>8–15 weeks: the most sensitive time for intellectual disability (threshold ≈ 300 mGy).<br>Throughout: an increased childhood cancer risk.',
    'ICRP 84: fetal doses below 100 mGy are not a reason to terminate a pregnancy. Most diagnostic exams give well under 50 mGy.'],
  ['rbio18', 'Define D₀, n and D<sub>q</sub> in the multitarget survival model.',
    'D₀: the dose that reduces survival to 37% on the straight (exponential) part of the curve.<br>n: the extrapolation number.<br>D<sub>q</sub>: the quasi-threshold dose, the width of the shoulder. D<sub>q</sub> = D₀ ln n.',
    'A broad shoulder means more capacity to repair sublethal damage, and more sparing from fractionation.'],
  ['rbio19', 'Write the Poisson tumor control probability.',
    'TCP = e<sup>−N × SF</sup>, where N is the number of clonogens and SF the surviving fraction.',
    'TCP = 37% when an average of one clonogen survives. Because TCP curves are steep, small dose changes near the middle matter a lot.'],
  ['rbio20', 'What is the dose-rate effect? The inverse dose-rate effect?',
    'Dose-rate effect: lowering the dose rate (from about 1 Gy/min to 0.01 Gy/min) reduces cell killing, because sublethal damage is repaired during the exposure.<br>Inverse effect: in some cell lines, a narrow range of lower dose rates kills more, because cells are arrested in sensitive G2.',
    'This is the basis of the biological differences between LDR and HDR brachytherapy.'],
  ['rbio21', 'How fast is sublethal damage repaired, and what does that mean for fractionation?',
    'Repair half-time is about 1–2 hours (longer in some late-responding tissues, like spinal cord).',
    'Allow at least 6 hours between fractions in hyperfractionated or twice-daily schedules.'],
  ['rbio22', 'What is accelerated repopulation, and what is its practical consequence?',
    'Surviving tumor clonogens divide faster after treatment starts, beginning about 3–4 weeks into a course for head and neck cancer.',
    'Each extra day of treatment loses roughly 0.5–0.6 Gy of effective dose, so avoid unplanned breaks and consider compensating for them.'],
  ['rbio23', 'Contrast serial and parallel organs, with examples.',
    'Serial: damage to any part disables the whole (spinal cord, brainstem, optic nerves, bowel), so the maximum dose matters.<br>Parallel: functional units work independently (lung, liver, kidney, parotid), so mean dose and volume metrics matter.',
    'This is why constraints are written as D<sub>max</sub> for the cord but as mean dose or V<sub>20</sub> for the lung.'],
  ['rbio24', 'Give examples of radiosensitizers and radioprotectors.',
    'Hypoxic-cell sensitizers: nimorazole, misonidazole (oxygen mimics). Also chemotherapy agents such as cisplatin and 5-FU.<br>Radioprotector: amifostine (a free-radical scavenger), used to reduce xerostomia.',
    'Halogenated pyrimidines (BUdR, IUdR) sensitize by being incorporated into DNA.'],
  ['rbio25', 'State the law of Bergonié and Tribondeau.',
    'Cells are more radiosensitive when they divide more often, have a longer dividing future, and are less differentiated.',
    'Lymphocytes are the notable exception: though they don’t divide, they are among the most radiosensitive cells (they die in interphase by apoptosis), so lymphocyte counts are an early dosimeter after an accident.'],
  ['rbio26', 'Give typical LET values for Co-60 gammas, 250 kV x-rays, protons and alphas.',
    'Co-60: ≈ 0.2 keV/µm<br>250 kV x-rays: ≈ 2 keV/µm<br>10 MeV protons: ≈ 4.7 keV/µm<br>2.5 MeV alphas: ≈ 166 keV/µm',
    'Therapeutic protons are low-LET for most of their path; LET rises sharply at the distal edge of the Bragg peak.'],

  // ───── Radiation protection ─────
  ['prot01', 'Define equivalent dose and effective dose.',
    'Equivalent dose H<sub>T</sub> = Σ w<sub>R</sub> × D<sub>T,R</sub> (Sv)<br>Effective dose E = Σ w<sub>T</sub> × H<sub>T</sub> (Sv)',
    'Effective dose is a protection quantity for a reference person, used to compare stochastic risk. It is not meant for individual patient risk estimates.'],
  ['prot02', 'Give the ICRP 103 radiation weighting factors.',
    'Photons, electrons, muons: 1<br>Protons (and charged pions): 2<br>Alphas, fission fragments, heavy ions: 20<br>Neutrons: a continuous function of energy, 2.5–20 (peaking near 1 MeV)',
    'ICRP 60 had given protons 5 and neutrons a step function.'],
  ['prot03', 'Give the ICRP 103 tissue weighting factors.',
    '0.12: red bone marrow, colon, lung, stomach, breast, remainder tissues<br>0.08: gonads<br>0.04: bladder, esophagus, liver, thyroid<br>0.01: bone surface, brain, salivary glands, skin',
    'They sum to 1. ICRP 103 cut gonads from 0.20 (ICRP 60) to 0.08 and raised breast from 0.05 to 0.12.'],
  ['prot04', 'What are the NRC occupational dose limits (10 CFR 20)?',
    'Total effective dose equivalent: 50 mSv (5 rem) per year<br>Lens of the eye: 150 mSv (15 rem)<br>Skin, extremities, or any single organ: 500 mSv (50 rem)<br>Minors: 10% of the adult limits<br>Embryo/fetus of a declared pregnant worker: 5 mSv (0.5 rem) over the pregnancy',
    'Doses from medical care and natural background don’t count toward these limits.'],
  ['prot05', 'What are the NRC dose limits for members of the public?',
    '1 mSv (100 mrem) per year total effective dose equivalent, and no more than 0.02 mSv (2 mrem) in any one hour in an unrestricted area.',
    'Visitors of patients with radioactive material may receive up to 5 mSv if authorized (10 CFR 20.1301).'],
  ['prot06', 'What occupational limits do ICRP and NCRP recommend?',
    'ICRP 103: 20 mSv/yr averaged over 5 years, no more than 50 mSv in any year; lens 20 mSv/yr; skin and extremities 500 mSv; public 1 mSv/yr.<br>NCRP 116: 50 mSv/yr, with a cumulative limit of 10 mSv × age in years; embryo/fetus 0.5 mSv per month once the pregnancy is known.',
    'NCRP 180 (2018) has since recommended a lens limit of 50 mSv/yr in the US.'],
  ['prot07', 'About how much natural background radiation does a person in the US receive?',
    'About 3 mSv per year, of which radon and thoron give about 2.3 mSv.',
    'NCRP 160 put the total US average, including medical exposure, at 6.2 mSv/yr (2006); NCRP 184 found medical exposure had fallen to about 2.2 mSv per person by 2016.'],
  ['prot08', 'Write the primary barrier transmission equation and how to get a thickness from it.',
    'B = P d² / (W U T)<br>Number of TVLs n = log₁₀(1/B)<br>Thickness = TVL₁ + (n − 1) × TVL<sub>e</sub>',
    'P: design goal (Sv/week); d: distance from the source to the point protected (m); W: workload (Gy/week at 1 m); U: use factor; T: occupancy factor.'],
  ['prot09', 'What shielding design goals do NCRP 147 and 151 use?',
    'Controlled areas: 0.1 mSv/week (5 mSv/yr)<br>Uncontrolled areas: 0.02 mSv/week (1 mSv/yr)',
    'NCRP 151 also checks the time-averaged dose rate (TADR) and the instantaneous dose rate for uncontrolled areas.'],
  ['prot10', 'Give typical occupancy factors from NCRP 147/151.',
    '1: offices, labs, nursing stations, control areas<br>1/2: patient exam and treatment rooms<br>1/5: corridors, patient rooms<br>1/20: restrooms, storage, unattended vending<br>1/40: outdoors, unattended parking, stairways',
    'Occupancy is for the most-exposed person who is there routinely, not for the space as a whole.'],
  ['prot11', 'What leakage limit is assumed for a linac head, and what use factor do secondary barriers get?',
    'Head leakage ≤ 0.1% of the useful beam dose, at 1 m from the target. Secondary barriers always have U = 1.',
    'Secondary barriers protect against leakage and patient scatter; scatter is much softer and is calculated with scatter fractions at given angles.'],
  ['prot12', 'Give approximate concrete TVLs for 6 MV and 18 MV primary beams.',
    '6 MV: TVL₁ ≈ 37 cm, TVL<sub>e</sub> ≈ 33 cm<br>18 MV: TVL₁ ≈ 45 cm, TVL<sub>e</sub> ≈ 43 cm<br>(ordinary concrete, 2.35 g/cm³, NCRP 151)',
    'Lead TVLs are about 5.7 cm for both. High-density concrete or steel saves space, but lead and steel don’t moderate neutrons.'],
  ['prot13', 'Define a radiation area, high radiation area and very high radiation area (NRC).',
    'Radiation area: > 0.05 mSv (5 mrem) in 1 hour at 30 cm<br>High radiation area: > 1 mSv (100 mrem) in 1 hour at 30 cm<br>Very high radiation area: > 5 Gy (500 rad) in 1 hour at 1 m',
    'Each must be posted; high and very high radiation areas also need access controls.'],
  ['prot14', 'What are the criteria for DOT radioactive package labels?',
    'White-I: surface ≤ 0.005 mSv/h (0.5 mrem/h); TI = 0<br>Yellow-II: surface ≤ 0.5 mSv/h (50 mrem/h); TI ≤ 1<br>Yellow-III: surface ≤ 2 mSv/h (200 mrem/h); TI ≤ 10',
    'The transport index (TI) is the maximum dose rate at 1 m in mrem/h. A package must be labeled for the higher category that either criterion requires.'],
  ['prot15', 'What is a medical event under NRC 10 CFR 35.3045?',
    'Among others: the total dose delivered differs from the prescribed dose by 20% or more, or a single fraction differs by 50% or more; or the wrong patient, drug, route or treatment site — each provided the error also exceeds 0.05 Sv effective dose, 0.5 Sv to an organ or tissue, or 0.5 Sv shallow dose to the skin.',
    'Permanent implants have separate criteria based on source strength (activity) rather than dose.'],
  ['prot16', 'How quickly must a medical event be reported?',
    'Telephone the NRC Operations Center no later than the next calendar day; a written report within 15 days. Tell the referring physician, and the patient (unless the physician decides it would be harmful), within 24 hours.',
    'Agreement States have equivalent rules.'],
  ['prot17', 'When does the NRC require a written directive?',
    'Before giving I-131 sodium iodide above 1.11 MBq (30 µCi), any therapeutic dosage of unsealed byproduct material, and any HDR, gamma stereotactic, teletherapy or permanent-implant brachytherapy treatment.',
    'An authorized user must date and sign it. For permanent implants, part is written before and part after the implant.'],
  ['prot18', 'When can a patient given radioactive material be released (10 CFR 35.75)?',
    'When the total effective dose to any other person is unlikely to exceed 5 mSv (0.5 rem). Written instructions are needed if it could exceed 1 mSv (0.1 rem).',
    'For I-131, NUREG-1556 Vol. 9 allows release at or below 1.22 GBq (33 mCi) retained, or a dose rate at or below 0.07 mSv/h (7 mrem/h) at 1 m, or by a patient-specific calculation.'],
  ['prot19', 'How often must sealed sources be leak tested, and what counts as leaking?',
    'Every 6 months. A source with 185 Bq (0.005 µCi) or more of removable contamination is leaking: take it out of use and report it.',
    'Sources with short half-lives, low activity, or that are gases are exempt.'],
  ['prot20', 'How is effective dose estimated from badges worn with a lead apron?',
    'Two badges (NCRP 122): E = 0.5 H<sub>W</sub> + 0.025 H<sub>N</sub>, where H<sub>W</sub> is under the apron at the waist and H<sub>N</sub> is over it at the collar.<br>One collar badge over the apron: E ≈ 0.3 H<sub>N</sub>.',
    'The collar badge also estimates the lens and thyroid doses.'],
  ['prot21', 'How much protection does a lead apron give?',
    'A 0.5 mm lead-equivalent apron stops about 95–99% of scattered radiation at typical fluoroscopy energies; 0.25 mm is the usual regulatory minimum.',
    'Aprons should be checked (often fluoroscopically) about once a year for cracks.'],
  ['prot22', 'What is a handy rule of thumb for scatter in fluoroscopy, and where is it highest?',
    'Scatter at 1 m from the patient is about 1/1000 (0.1%) of the entrance skin dose. It is highest on the x-ray tube (entrance) side.',
    'So keep the tube under the table and stand on the detector side for lateral views.'],
  ['prot23', 'What are the three principles of radiation protection (ICRP)?',
    'Justification: the benefit must outweigh the harm.<br>Optimization: keep doses as low as reasonably achievable (ALARA).<br>Limitation: individual dose limits (which don’t apply to patients).',
    'The practical tools are time, distance and shielding.'],
  ['prot24', 'What is a diagnostic reference level?',
    'A benchmark dose for a common exam, usually the 75th percentile of the doses in a survey. It is not a limit.',
    'Facilities whose typical doses exceed the DRL should review their protocols. Achievable doses are often set near the median.'],
  ['prot25', 'Give typical effective doses for common exams.',
    'Chest x-ray (PA): 0.02 mSv · Mammogram (screening): 0.4 mSv<br>Head CT: 2 mSv · Chest CT: 7 mSv · Abdomen–pelvis CT: 8–10 mSv<br>FDG PET: ≈ 7 mSv for 370 MBq (0.019 mSv/MBq)',
    'Compare with about 3 mSv per year from natural background. Values follow Mettler et al. (2008) and vary widely with technique and patient size.'],

  // ───── Radiography, fluoro & CT ─────
  ['xray01', 'What is the line-focus principle?',
    'Effective focal spot length = actual length × sin(anode angle). A steep anode (7–20°) gives a small effective spot while spreading the heat over a larger area.',
    'The trade-off: smaller angles limit field coverage and strengthen the heel effect.'],
  ['xray02', 'What is the heel effect, and how is it used?',
    'X-ray intensity is lower on the anode side of the field because photons leaving toward the anode cross more target material.',
    'Put thicker anatomy toward the cathode (e.g. in mammography, the chest wall). The effect is stronger with smaller anode angles, shorter SIDs and larger fields.'],
  ['xray03', 'How does x-ray tube output depend on mAs and kVp?',
    'Output is proportional to mAs and roughly proportional to kVp² (higher at the detector, around kVp⁴–kVp⁵, after the patient).',
    'Output also rises with target Z.'],
  ['xray04', 'State the 15% rule.',
    'A 15% increase in kVp gives about the same detector exposure as doubling the mAs.',
    'So raising kVp 15% and halving mAs keeps the image receptor exposure about the same, at lower patient dose but with less subject contrast.'],
  ['xray05', 'What minimum total filtration does the FDA require for general radiography?',
    'At least 2.5 mm Al equivalent for tubes operating above 70 kVp.',
    'Compliance is shown by measuring HVL against FDA minimums (21 CFR 1020.30), e.g. about 2.9 mm Al at 80 kVp for newer equipment.'],
  ['xray06', 'Define grid ratio. What are the trade-offs of a higher ratio?',
    'Grid ratio = h/D, lead strip height over the interspace width.<br>Higher ratio: better scatter rejection, but higher patient dose (Bucky factor) and more grid cutoff from misalignment.',
    'Bucky factors are typically 2–6. Grids are generally not needed for thin parts (≲ 10 cm) or small children.'],
  ['xray07', 'How are magnification and geometric unsharpness calculated?',
    'M = SID / SOD<br>Geometric blur (penumbra) = f × (M − 1), with f the focal spot size.',
    'Keep the patient close to the detector and use a small focal spot to reduce blur. Magnification mammography deliberately uses the 0.1 mm spot.'],
  ['xray08', 'What is the air-gap technique?',
    'Moving the detector away from the patient so that obliquely scattered photons miss it, rejecting scatter without a grid.',
    'It increases magnification and blur, and needs more output; used in magnification mammography and lateral cervical spine views.'],
  ['xray09', 'What are the key physics features of mammography?',
    'Low kVp (≈ 25–35) with Mo, Rh, Ag or W targets and Mo, Rh, Ag or Al filters; focal spots of 0.3 mm (contact) and 0.1 mm (magnification); firm compression; high-resolution detectors.',
    'MQSA limits the mean glandular dose to 3 mGy per view for a 4.2 cm, 50/50 breast (ACR phantom). Compression reduces thickness, scatter, dose, motion and overlap.'],
  ['xray10', 'What does MQSA require of a mammography facility?',
    'FDA certification after accreditation, a qualified interpreting physician, technologists and medical physicist, an annual survey by the physicist, ongoing QC (including phantom images), and auditing of outcomes.',
    'Mammography equipment evaluations by a physicist are also required after installation or major repairs.'],
  ['xray11', 'What are the FDA limits on fluoroscopic air kerma rate?',
    'Normal mode: 88 mGy/min (10 R/min).<br>High-level control: 176 mGy/min (20 R/min), only with continuous activation and an audible signal.',
    'Measured at the reference point. Recording modes (cine, DSA) aren’t limited.'],
  ['xray12', 'Where is the interventional reference point?',
    'On the central axis, 15 cm from isocenter toward the focal spot.',
    'Systems must display the cumulative reference air kerma (K<sub>a,r</sub>) and kerma-area product. Because the patient’s skin is rarely there and the beam moves, K<sub>a,r</sub> overestimates or underestimates peak skin dose.'],
  ['xray13', 'What fluoroscopy dose is a Joint Commission sentinel event?',
    'A cumulative peak skin dose above 15 Gy to a single field.',
    'Many facilities follow up patients whose K<sub>a,r</sub> exceeds 5 Gy, or peak skin dose exceeds 3 Gy (SIR/NCRP 168 notification levels).'],
  ['xray14', 'What sets the brightness gain of an image intensifier?',
    'Brightness gain = minification gain × flux gain<br>Minification gain = (D<sub>input</sub>/D<sub>output</sub>)²',
    'Flux gain (about 50) comes from accelerating photoelectrons. Conversion factor (cd/m² per µGy/s) is the more modern measure.'],
  ['xray15', 'What happens to dose and resolution in a fluoroscopy magnification mode?',
    'Resolution improves, but the entrance dose rate rises, roughly by (FOV<sub>large</sub>/FOV<sub>small</sub>)² for an image intensifier (less for flat panels, which usually rise more gently).',
    'The automatic brightness system raises output to keep the image bright from a smaller input area.'],
  ['xray16', 'List the main ways to reduce dose in fluoroscopy.',
    'Pulsed fluoro at low frame rates, last-image hold, tight collimation, keeping the detector close to the patient and the tube far away, avoiding magnification, low-dose modes, removing the grid for small patients, and spreading skin dose by changing angles.',
    'Most of these also reduce staff dose, which comes mainly from patient scatter.'],
  ['xray17', 'Compare indirect and direct flat-panel detectors.',
    'Indirect: a scintillator (usually CsI:Tl) makes light, which a-Si photodiodes and TFTs read. Some light spread reduces resolution.<br>Direct: a photoconductor (a-Se) converts x-rays straight to charge, steered by an electric field, giving higher resolution.',
    'Direct a-Se is common in mammography; indirect CsI is common in radiography and fluoroscopy because of its higher absorption at higher energies.'],
  ['xray18', 'What is the Nyquist frequency of a digital detector?',
    'f<sub>N</sub> = 1 / (2 × pixel pitch). For a 100 µm pitch, f<sub>N</sub> = 5 lp/mm.',
    'Signals above f<sub>N</sub> alias to lower frequencies.'],
  ['xray19', 'Define the Hounsfield unit and give typical values.',
    'HU = 1000 × (μ − μ<sub>water</sub>) / μ<sub>water</sub><br>Air −1000 · Fat ≈ −100 · Water 0 · Soft tissue +20 to +60 · Bone +400 to +1000 or more',
    'HU depends on the beam energy for anything not water-like, so values shift with kVp and filtration.'],
  ['xray20', 'Define CTDI<sub>w</sub>, CTDI<sub>vol</sub> and DLP.',
    'CTDI<sub>w</sub> = ⅓ CTDI<sub>100,center</sub> + ⅔ CTDI<sub>100,periphery</sub><br>CTDI<sub>vol</sub> = CTDI<sub>w</sub> / pitch<br>DLP = CTDI<sub>vol</sub> × scan length (mGy·cm)',
    'Measured in 16 cm (head) or 32 cm (body) PMMA phantoms with a 100 mm pencil chamber. CTDI<sub>vol</sub> describes the scanner output, not a patient’s dose.'],
  ['xray21', 'Define CT pitch.',
    'Pitch = table travel per rotation ÷ total nominal beam width (N × T).',
    'Pitch < 1 overlaps (higher dose); pitch > 1 leaves gaps that interpolation covers. Many scanners raise mA automatically with pitch to keep noise constant.'],
  ['xray22', 'How is effective dose estimated from DLP? Give typical k factors.',
    'E ≈ k × DLP<br>Adult head: 0.0021 · Chest: 0.014 · Abdomen–pelvis: 0.015 mSv/(mGy·cm)',
    'From AAPM Report 96. For example, a chest CT with DLP 400 mGy·cm gives about 5.6 mSv.'],
  ['xray23', 'What is the size-specific dose estimate (SSDE)?',
    'SSDE = CTDI<sub>vol</sub> × a conversion factor for the patient’s size, from effective diameter (AAPM TG-204) or water-equivalent diameter (TG-220).',
    'Smaller patients absorb more of the beam, so their SSDE is higher than the CTDI<sub>vol</sub> (32 cm phantom) shown on the console.'],
  ['xray24', 'How does CT noise depend on dose?',
    'Noise σ ∝ 1/√(photons) ∝ 1/√(mAs). Halving the noise needs four times the dose.',
    'Noise also falls with thicker slices and smoother reconstruction kernels, and rises with patient size.'],
  ['xray25', 'Name common CT artifacts and their causes.',
    'Ring: a miscalibrated or failed detector element<br>Cupping and dark streaks: beam hardening<br>Streaks: metal, photon starvation, motion<br>Partial volume: thick slices across tissue boundaries<br>Out-of-field: anatomy outside the scan field',
    'Fixes include calibration, beam-hardening and metal artifact reduction algorithms, higher kVp, and thinner slices.'],
  ['xray26', 'What are the ACR CT accreditation CTDI<sub>vol</sub> limits?',
    'Pass/fail: adult head 80 mGy, adult abdomen 30 mGy, pediatric (5-year-old) abdomen 25 mGy.<br>Reference values: 75, 25 and 20 mGy.',
    'Measured for the site’s routine protocols, in the standard CTDI phantoms.'],
  ['xray27', 'How does CT tube current modulation work?',
    'mA is varied with angle (more through the long lateral path, less AP) and along z (more in the shoulders and pelvis, less in the lungs), usually based on the scout image, to keep image quality constant.',
    'Centering the patient in the gantry matters: off-center patients get a mis-sized scout and the wrong mA.'],
  ['xray28', 'How does filtered backprojection work, and what does iterative reconstruction add?',
    'FBP filters each projection with a ramp (Ram-Lak) filter, often with a smoothing window, then backprojects. Sharper kernels give more noise.<br>Iterative (and deep-learning) reconstruction models the noise and system, cutting noise and allowing lower doses.',
    'Strong iterative reconstruction can change the noise texture, giving a “plastic” look.'],
  ['xray29', 'How is dual-energy CT done, and what can it produce?',
    'Two spectra, from dual sources, fast kV switching, a dual-layer detector, or a split filter.<br>Outputs: material decomposition, iodine maps, virtual non-contrast and virtual monoenergetic images, and uric acid vs calcium stone typing.',
    'It works because photoelectric absorption depends strongly on Z and energy, while Compton scattering does not.'],
  ['xray30', 'What is photon-counting CT?',
    'Detectors (CdTe or Si) count individual photons and sort them by energy, with no electronic noise and smaller pixels.',
    'Gives inherent spectral imaging, better spatial resolution, and better iodine contrast at lower dose.'],

  // ───── Imaging science & statistics ─────
  ['imgs01', 'Define sensitivity and specificity.',
    'Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN): the fraction of diseased people who test positive.<br>Specificity = TN / (TN + FP): the fraction of healthy people who test negative.',
    'Neither depends on disease prevalence.'],
  ['imgs02', 'Define positive and negative predictive value.',
    'PPV = TP / (TP + FP)<br>NPV = TN / (TN + FN)',
    'Both depend on prevalence: in a screening population with low prevalence, even a good test has a low PPV.'],
  ['imgs03', 'What does an ROC curve plot, and what does its area mean?',
    'True positive fraction (sensitivity) vs false positive fraction (1 − specificity), as the decision threshold changes.',
    'AUC = 0.5 is chance; 1.0 is perfect. AUC is the probability that a randomly chosen diseased case scores higher than a randomly chosen healthy one.'],
  ['imgs04', 'What is the uncertainty in a count of N under Poisson statistics?',
    'σ = √N; the relative uncertainty is 1/√N.',
    'For 1% precision you need 10,000 counts; for 10%, only 100.'],
  ['imgs05', 'How do uncertainties combine for the sum or difference of two counts? For a count rate?',
    'Sum or difference: σ = √(σ₁² + σ₂²) = √(N₁ + N₂).<br>Rate R = N/t: σ<sub>R</sub> = √N / t = √(R/t).',
    'For net counts (gross − background), the uncertainty is √(gross + background), always larger than √(net).'],
  ['imgs06', 'What is the MTF, and how does it relate to the PSF, LSF and ESF?',
    'The modulation transfer function describes how well each spatial frequency is transferred: MTF(f) = |Fourier transform of the LSF|, normalized to 1 at f = 0.',
    'The LSF is the derivative of the ESF (edge spread function) and an integral of the PSF. The MTF of a chain of components is the product of their MTFs.'],
  ['imgs07', 'Define detective quantum efficiency (DQE).',
    'DQE = SNR²<sub>out</sub> / SNR²<sub>in</sub>, measuring how efficiently a detector uses the incident photons. An ideal detector has DQE = 1.',
    'DQE(f) = MTF²(f) / (q × NNPS(f)), where q is the photon fluence and NNPS the normalized noise power spectrum.'],
  ['imgs08', 'What does the noise power spectrum describe?',
    'How the noise variance is spread over spatial frequency: its texture (fine or blotchy), not just its magnitude.',
    'Two images with the same standard deviation can look very different if their NPS differ, for example with iterative reconstruction in CT.'],
  ['imgs09', 'What is the Rose criterion?',
    'An object needs a signal-to-noise ratio of about 5 to be detected reliably.',
    'In the Rose model, SNR = C√(Φ A), with C the contrast, Φ the photon fluence and A the object area, so low-contrast objects need many more photons.'],
  ['imgs10', 'Define contrast-to-noise ratio.',
    'CNR = |S<sub>A</sub> − S<sub>B</sub>| / σ, the signal difference between two regions over the noise.',
    'CNR is the common figure of merit for low-contrast detectability in phantom tests.'],
  ['imgs11', 'How is a line-pair resolution limit related to bar width?',
    'One line pair = one bar plus one space. Resolution (lp/mm) = 1 / (2 × bar width in mm).',
    'For example, 0.1 mm bars and spaces are 5 lp/mm.'],
  ['imgs12', 'What are Type I and Type II errors? Power?',
    'Type I (α): a false positive, rejecting a true null hypothesis.<br>Type II (β): a false negative, failing to reject a false null hypothesis.<br>Power = 1 − β.',
    'Power rises with sample size, effect size and α.'],
  ['imgs13', 'What is the standard error of the mean?',
    'SEM = σ / √n',
    'It describes the uncertainty of the mean, not the spread of the data. A 95% confidence interval is about the mean ± 1.96 SEM.'],
  ['imgs14', 'What fraction of a normal distribution lies within 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations?',
    '±1σ: 68.3% · ±2σ: 95.4% · ±3σ: 99.7%',
    '95% lies within ±1.96σ.'],
  ['imgs15', 'Why is a chi-square test used on radiation counters?',
    'To check that repeated counts scatter as Poisson statistics predict. Too much or too little variation points to a faulty instrument.',
    'χ² = Σ(N<sub>i</sub> − N̄)² / N̄, compared with tables for n − 1 degrees of freedom.'],
  ['imgs16', 'How many gray levels does an n-bit image have?',
    '2ⁿ: 8 bits = 256, 10 bits = 1024, 12 bits = 4096, 16 bits = 65,536.',
    'CT values (−1024 to +3071 HU) fit in 12 bits.'],
  ['imgs17', 'How do you find pixel size from FOV and matrix?',
    'Pixel size = FOV / matrix. A 25.6 cm FOV with a 512 matrix gives 0.5 mm pixels.',
    'Voxel size adds slice thickness. Smaller pixels improve resolution only if the system can support it, and they raise noise per pixel.'],
  ['imgs18', 'Give typical CT window settings.',
    'Soft tissue: W 400, L 40<br>Lung: W 1500, L −600<br>Bone: W ≈ 1800–2000, L ≈ 400–500<br>Brain: W 80, L 40',
    'Narrow windows show subtle contrast; wide windows show a wide range of tissues at once.'],
  ['imgs19', 'What is the convolution theorem, and why does it matter in imaging?',
    'Convolution in the spatial domain is multiplication in the frequency domain.',
    'So blurring by a PSF multiplies the image spectrum by the MTF, and filtered backprojection can apply its ramp filter by multiplication in Fourier space.'],
  ['imgs20', 'Define the positive likelihood ratio.',
    'LR+ = sensitivity / (1 − specificity)',
    'Post-test odds = pre-test odds × LR. An LR+ above 10 strongly rules in disease; an LR− below 0.1 strongly rules it out.'],
  ['imgs21', 'Define accuracy for a diagnostic test.',
    'Accuracy = (TP + TN) / total',
    'Accuracy depends on prevalence, which can make it misleading: a test that calls everyone healthy is 99% accurate when prevalence is 1%.'],

  // ───── MRI & ultrasound ─────
  ['mrus01', 'Write the Larmor equation and the gyromagnetic ratio of hydrogen.',
    'f = (γ/2π) × B₀; for ¹H, γ/2π = 42.58 MHz/T.<br>1.5 T → 63.9 MHz; 3 T → 127.7 MHz',
    'Gradients make the frequency vary with position, which is how MRI encodes space.'],
  ['mrus02', 'Define T1, T2 and T2*.',
    'T1 (spin–lattice): time for M<sub>z</sub> to recover to 63% of its equilibrium value.<br>T2 (spin–spin): time for M<sub>xy</sub> to decay to 37%.<br>T2*: the observed decay of M<sub>xy</sub>, adding field inhomogeneity; T2* < T2 ≤ T1.',
    'T1 lengthens with field strength; T2 changes little. Water has long T1 and T2; fat has short T1.'],
  ['mrus03', 'How do TR and TE set T1, T2 and proton-density weighting in spin echo?',
    'T1-weighted: short TR, short TE<br>T2-weighted: long TR, long TE<br>Proton density: long TR, short TE',
    'TR controls T1 weighting; TE controls T2 weighting.'],
  ['mrus04', 'How do spin echo and gradient echo differ?',
    'Spin echo uses a 90° pulse and a 180° refocusing pulse, which cancels static inhomogeneity, giving T2 contrast.<br>Gradient echo uses a smaller flip angle and gradient reversal only, so it is faster but T2*-weighted.',
    'For spoiled gradient echo, the Ernst angle maximizes signal: cos θ<sub>E</sub> = e<sup>−TR/T1</sup>.'],
  ['mrus05', 'How do STIR and FLAIR work?',
    'Inversion recovery: a 180° pulse, then readout at the inversion time TI when a tissue’s M<sub>z</sub> crosses zero, nulling it. TI<sub>null</sub> = T1 × ln 2 ≈ 0.693 T1.<br>STIR nulls fat (short TI ≈ 150–170 ms at 1.5 T); FLAIR nulls CSF (long TI ≈ 2000+ ms).',
    'STIR fat suppression is robust to field inhomogeneity but also suppresses anything with a T1 like fat, including gadolinium-enhanced tissue.'],
  ['mrus06', 'What do the center and edges of k-space control?',
    'Center: low spatial frequencies, giving overall signal and contrast.<br>Periphery: high spatial frequencies, giving edges and fine detail.',
    'Every point in k-space contributes to every pixel in the image.'],
  ['mrus07', 'How long does a 2D spin-echo scan take?',
    'Time = TR × N<sub>phase</sub> × NEX, divided by the echo train length for fast (turbo) spin echo.',
    'For example, TR 2000 ms, 256 phase steps, NEX 2 gives 1024 s ≈ 17 min; with ETL 8, about 2 min.'],
  ['mrus08', 'How does MRI SNR depend on the scan parameters?',
    'SNR ∝ voxel volume × √(N<sub>phase</sub> × NEX / bandwidth), and roughly ∝ B₀.',
    'Doubling NEX gives √2 more SNR at twice the time. Halving the bandwidth gives √2 more SNR but more chemical shift.'],
  ['mrus09', 'What is the fat–water chemical shift?',
    '3.5 ppm: about 224 Hz at 1.5 T and 447 Hz at 3 T.',
    'Causes a chemical shift artifact (a bright and dark band) along the frequency-encoding direction, worse at higher field and lower bandwidth.'],
  ['mrus10', 'Name common MRI artifacts and their causes.',
    'Aliasing (wrap-around): FOV smaller than the anatomy, in the phase direction<br>Chemical shift: along the frequency direction<br>Gibbs (truncation): sharp edges with few phase steps<br>Susceptibility: metal or air, worst on gradient echo<br>Motion and flow: ghosts along the phase direction<br>Zipper: RF leaks into the room',
    'Fixes: larger FOV or oversampling, higher bandwidth, more matrix, spin echo, saturation bands, a closed RF door.'],
  ['mrus11', 'What are the SAR limits in MRI?',
    'Normal operating mode: whole body 2 W/kg; head 3.2 W/kg.<br>First-level controlled mode: whole body 4 W/kg.',
    'SAR scales with B₀² and flip angle², so 3 T scans and fast spin echo with many 180° pulses can hit the limits.'],
  ['mrus12', 'What are the ACR MRI safety zones?',
    'Zone I: public areas<br>Zone II: the interface where patients are screened<br>Zone III: restricted, under the control of MR personnel<br>Zone IV: the magnet room itself',
    'Ferromagnetic objects in Zone IV can become projectiles; the static field is always on, even during a power cut.'],
  ['mrus13', 'What are the main MRI safety hazards?',
    'Static field: projectiles, torque on implants<br>Time-varying gradients (dB/dt): peripheral nerve stimulation, acoustic noise<br>RF: heating (SAR), burns from loops and leads<br>Cryogens: quench, asphyxia',
    'Patients need hearing protection; device labels are MR Safe, MR Conditional or MR Unsafe.'],
  ['mrus14', 'What is a quench?',
    'A sudden loss of superconductivity: the magnet warms, and liquid helium boils off rapidly and is vented through the quench pipe.',
    'If the vent fails, helium can displace oxygen in the room. A quench is used only for emergencies such as a person pinned by a ferromagnetic object.'],
  ['mrus15', 'How does gadolinium contrast work?',
    'Its paramagnetic ions shorten T1 (and T2) of nearby water, so enhancing tissue is bright on T1-weighted images.',
    'Risks include nephrogenic systemic fibrosis with older agents in renal failure, and gadolinium retention in the brain.'],
  ['mrus16', 'What is the speed of sound in soft tissue, and how is depth found from echo time?',
    'c ≈ 1540 m/s, so the round trip takes 13 µs per cm of depth. Depth = c × t / 2.',
    'Bone (~4080 m/s) and air (~330 m/s) are very different, which causes speed-of-sound artifacts.'],
  ['mrus17', 'Define acoustic impedance and the intensity reflection coefficient.',
    'Z = ρ × c (rayls)<br>R = [(Z₂ − Z₁) / (Z₂ + Z₁)]²',
    'Soft tissue–air reflects about 99.9%, so coupling gel is needed. Soft tissue–bone reflects about 40%.'],
  ['mrus18', 'How much does soft tissue attenuate ultrasound?',
    'About 0.5 dB/cm/MHz (one way).',
    'Higher frequencies give better resolution but less penetration, so deep imaging uses lower frequencies (≈ 2–5 MHz) and superficial imaging higher ones (≈ 7–15 MHz).'],
  ['mrus19', 'What determines axial, lateral and elevational resolution?',
    'Axial: half the spatial pulse length (SPL/2); improved by higher frequency and damping.<br>Lateral: beam width, best at the focus.<br>Elevational: slice thickness, set by the array’s height and lens.',
    'Axial resolution is usually the best of the three.'],
  ['mrus20', 'How long is an unfocused transducer’s near field?',
    'Near-field (Fresnel) length = D² / (4λ) = r² / λ.',
    'Beyond it, the beam diverges in the far field (Fraunhofer zone). Larger transducers and higher frequencies give longer near fields.'],
  ['mrus21', 'Write the Doppler equation, and state when aliasing occurs.',
    'f<sub>D</sub> = 2 f₀ v cos θ / c<br>Aliasing in pulsed Doppler when f<sub>D</sub> > PRF/2 (the Nyquist limit).',
    'Keep the angle under 60° because cos θ changes quickly above it. Fix aliasing by raising the PRF, lowering f₀, shifting the baseline, or using continuous-wave Doppler.'],
  ['mrus22', 'What are the mechanical and thermal indices, and the FDA limits?',
    'MI = peak rarefactional pressure (MPa) / √f (MHz), the risk of cavitation; FDA limit 1.9.<br>TI = power / power needed for a 1 °C rise.<br>Derated I<sub>SPTA</sub> limit: 720 mW/cm².',
    'Use the lowest output for the job (ALARA), especially in obstetrics; TIs (soft tissue), TIb (bone) and TIc (cranial) apply to different targets.'],
  ['mrus23', 'How thick are a transducer’s piezoelectric element and matching layer?',
    'Element: λ/2 (thickness sets the resonant frequency).<br>Matching layer: λ/4, with an impedance between the crystal’s and the skin’s.',
    'A damping backing block shortens the pulse, improving axial resolution and widening the bandwidth.'],
  ['mrus24', 'Name common ultrasound artifacts and their causes.',
    'Reverberation and comet tail: repeated reflections between strong reflectors<br>Mirror image: a strong reflector like the diaphragm<br>Shadowing: strong attenuators (stones, bone)<br>Enhancement: weak attenuators (fluid-filled cysts)<br>Refraction, side and grating lobes: misplaced echoes',
    'Shadowing and enhancement are diagnostically useful.'],
  ['mrus25', 'What limits ultrasound frame rate?',
    'PRF<sub>max</sub> = c / (2 × depth); frame rate = PRF / lines per frame.',
    'Deeper imaging, more lines, or more focal zones lower the frame rate.'],
  ['mrus26', 'Give the MR spectroscopy chemical shifts of the main brain metabolites.',
    'NAA: 2.0 ppm (neurons) · Creatine: 3.0 ppm · Choline: 3.2 ppm (membranes) · Lactate: 1.3 ppm doublet (inverts at TE 144 ms)',
    'Tumors typically show raised choline and lowered NAA.'],

  // ───── Nuclear medicine & PET ─────
  ['nmed01', 'Give the key facts about Tc-99m and its generator.',
    'Tc-99m: T½ 6.0 h, 140 keV gamma, decays by isomeric transition.<br>Parent Mo-99: T½ 66 h. Generators are eluted daily, when Tc-99m is near its maximum (about 23 h).',
    'Mo-99 adsorbs on alumina; pertechnetate (TcO₄⁻) is eluted with saline.'],
  ['nmed02', 'What are the generator eluate purity limits?',
    'Mo-99 breakthrough: ≤ 0.15 µCi Mo-99 per mCi Tc-99m (0.15 kBq/MBq) at administration (NRC).<br>Aluminum: < 10 µg/mL (USP).',
    'Because Tc-99m decays faster, the Mo/Tc ratio grows with time after elution, so check that it is still acceptable when the dose is given.'],
  ['nmed03', 'Give the half-life and main emissions of F-18.',
    'T½ 110 min; positron emitter (97%), E<sub>max</sub> 0.634 MeV, giving 511 keV annihilation photons.',
    'Its low positron energy gives a short range in tissue (mean ≈ 0.6 mm), which is good for PET resolution.'],
  ['nmed04', 'Give the half-lives and main photon energies of I-131 and I-123.',
    'I-131: 8.0 d; β⁻ (E<sub>max</sub> 606 keV) and a 364 keV gamma; used for therapy.<br>I-123: 13.2 h; electron capture, 159 keV gamma; used for imaging.',
    'I-131’s high-energy gamma needs a high-energy collimator and gives poorer images.'],
  ['nmed05', 'Give the half-lives and photon energies of Tl-201, Ga-67, In-111 and Xe-133.',
    'Tl-201: 73 h; 69–83 keV Hg x-rays (plus 135, 167 keV)<br>Ga-67: 78 h; 93, 185, 300 keV<br>In-111: 2.8 d; 171, 245 keV<br>Xe-133: 5.2 d; 81 keV',
    'Multi-peak nuclides (Ga-67, In-111) are imaged with several energy windows and medium-energy collimators.'],
  ['nmed06', 'Give the half-lives of the common PET nuclides besides F-18.',
    'Ga-68: 68 min (from a Ge-68 generator) · Rb-82: 75 s (from a Sr-82 generator) · N-13: 10 min · O-15: 2 min · C-11: 20 min · Cu-64: 12.7 h · Zr-89: 78 h',
    'Generator-produced nuclides (Ga-68, Rb-82) don’t need an on-site cyclotron.'],
  ['nmed07', 'Give the key facts about common therapeutic nuclides besides I-131.',
    'Lu-177: 6.65 d; β⁻ plus 113 and 208 keV gammas (imageable); used for PSMA and DOTATATE therapy.<br>Ra-223: 11.4 d; alpha emitter for bone metastases.<br>Y-90: 64 h (2.7 d); pure β⁻ (E<sub>max</sub> 2.28 MeV), used in microspheres for liver tumors.',
    'Y-90 is imaged through its bremsstrahlung (SPECT) or a tiny positron branch (PET).'],
  ['nmed08', 'What is the gamma camera crystal, and why?',
    'NaI(Tl), typically about 9.5 mm (⅜ inch) thick, viewed by an array of PMTs (or SiPMs).',
    'NaI(Tl) has high light output and good stopping at 140 keV. Thicker crystals improve sensitivity at higher energies but blur intrinsic resolution.'],
  ['nmed09', 'Compare gamma camera collimators.',
    'Parallel-hole: no magnification; LEHR for resolution, LEAP or high-sensitivity for counts.<br>Pinhole: magnifies small organs (thyroid), with low sensitivity.<br>Converging: magnifies; diverging: minifies to cover a larger organ.',
    'For a parallel-hole collimator, resolution worsens with distance, but sensitivity stays about constant (in air).'],
  ['nmed10', 'How do intrinsic and collimator resolution combine?',
    'System resolution R<sub>s</sub> = √(R<sub>i</sub>² + R<sub>c</sub>²).',
    'At typical imaging distances, collimator resolution dominates, so keep the camera as close to the patient as possible.'],
  ['nmed11', 'What gamma camera QC is done, and how often?',
    'Daily: energy peaking; uniformity flood (intrinsic with a point source, or extrinsic with a Co-57 sheet).<br>Weekly: spatial resolution and linearity with a bar phantom.<br>SPECT: center of rotation and high-count uniformity, periodically.',
    'Integral uniformity = (max − min)/(max + min) × 100%, in the useful field of view.'],
  ['nmed12', 'What energy window is typical for Tc-99m imaging?',
    'A 20% window centered on 140 keV: 126–154 keV.',
    'Narrower windows reject more scatter but lose counts.'],
  ['nmed13', 'Compare paralyzable and non-paralyzable dead time.',
    'Non-paralyzable: events during dead time are lost but don’t extend it; the measured rate levels off.<br>Paralyzable: each event restarts the dead time, so the measured rate peaks and then falls as true rates rise further.',
    'Gamma cameras behave as paralyzable, so a very hot source can give misleadingly low count rates.'],
  ['nmed14', 'What are the types of PET coincidences?',
    'True: both photons from one annihilation, unscattered.<br>Scatter: one or both photons scattered, misplacing the line of response.<br>Random: two unrelated photons within the timing window, rate R = 2τS₁S₂.',
    'Randoms rise with the square of activity; they are estimated with a delayed window and subtracted.'],
  ['nmed15', 'Compare common PET scintillators.',
    'BGO: high density and Z (good stopping), but slow and dim.<br>LSO/LYSO: dense, bright and fast; enables time of flight; slightly radioactive (Lu-176).<br>NaI(Tl): too low stopping power for 511 keV in modern PET.',
    'Newer systems pair LYSO with silicon photomultipliers (SiPMs) for better timing.'],
  ['nmed16', 'What does time of flight do in PET?',
    'It localizes each annihilation along the line of response to within Δx = cΔt / 2; 500 ps gives about 7.5 cm.',
    'This doesn’t sharpen resolution directly, but it reduces noise, especially in large patients.'],
  ['nmed17', 'Define the standardized uptake value.',
    'SUV = tissue activity concentration (kBq/mL) ÷ [injected activity (kBq) / body weight (g)], decay-corrected.',
    'SUV is affected by uptake time, blood glucose, residual activity in the syringe, extravasation, partial-volume effects, and reconstruction settings.'],
  ['nmed18', 'What limits PET spatial resolution?',
    'Detector size, positron range, photon non-collinearity (worse for larger rings), and depth-of-interaction (parallax) error.',
    'Positron range matters for high-energy emitters such as Rb-82 and Ga-68 much more than for F-18.'],
  ['nmed19', 'Write the MIRD dose equation and the cumulated activity for a simple case.',
    'D = Ã × S<br>Ã = A₀ × 1.44 × T<sub>eff</sub> (instant uptake, single-exponential clearance)',
    'S (Gy/Bq·s) is the absorbed dose to a target per decay in a source organ, from MIRD tables or OLINDA.'],
  ['nmed20', 'What is the effective half-life?',
    '1/T<sub>eff</sub> = 1/T<sub>p</sub> + 1/T<sub>b</sub>, so T<sub>eff</sub> = T<sub>p</sub>T<sub>b</sub> / (T<sub>p</sub> + T<sub>b</sub>).',
    'T<sub>eff</sub> is always shorter than either the physical or biological half-life.'],
  ['nmed21', 'What QC is required for a dose calibrator?',
    'Constancy: daily · Linearity: quarterly · Accuracy: annually · Geometry: at installation and after repair.',
    'A dose calibrator is a pressurized argon well ionization chamber; settings are chosen per nuclide.'],
  ['nmed22', 'How is the lung shunt fraction used in Y-90 microsphere therapy?',
    'Tc-99m MAA mimics the microspheres; the fraction shunting to the lungs limits the activity. Lung dose limits: about 30 Gy per treatment and 50 Gy cumulative.',
    'MAA also shows any extrahepatic deposition (e.g. to the stomach) that must be prevented by embolizing vessels.'],
  ['nmed23', 'How does SPECT correct for attenuation?',
    'Older systems use the Chang method (assumes uniform attenuation); SPECT/CT uses a CT-based attenuation map, scaled from the CT energy to the emission energy.',
    'Misregistration between the CT and the emission data causes artifacts, for example on cardiac scans.'],
  ['nmed24', 'Name the main mechanisms of radiopharmaceutical localization.',
    'Active transport (I-131, Tl-201), metabolic trapping (FDG), capillary blockade (MAA), phagocytosis (sulfur colloid), receptor binding (DOTATATE, PSMA), compartmental localization (labeled RBCs), and chemisorption (MDP on bone).',
    'Knowing the mechanism explains normal distributions and artifacts.'],

  // ───── External beam therapy ─────
  ['ther01', 'Compare magnetrons and klystrons.',
    'Magnetron: an oscillator that generates microwaves; compact; used in lower-energy linacs (about 4–6 MV, up to ~10).<br>Klystron: an amplifier of low-power microwaves; higher power; used in higher-energy linacs.',
    'Clinical linacs mostly use S-band microwaves (about 3 GHz); compact and robotic units use X-band (about 9 GHz).'],
  ['ther02', 'Compare standing-wave and traveling-wave accelerating guides.',
    'Standing wave: microwaves reflect at both ends, forming a standing pattern; shorter guide, needs a circulator to protect the source.<br>Traveling wave: microwaves travel with the electrons and are absorbed at the end; longer guide.',
    'Side-coupled cavities in standing-wave guides keep the guide short.'],
  ['ther03', 'Why do linacs use a 270° bending magnet?',
    'It is achromatic: electrons with slightly different energies are refocused to the same spot on the target, so the beam spot stays small and stable.',
    'Low-energy linacs with short guides may have no bending magnet, the guide pointing straight at the patient.'],
  ['ther04', 'What does a flattening filter do, and how do FFF beams differ?',
    'It flattens a forward-peaked bremsstrahlung beam across the field.<br>FFF beams: higher dose rate (2–4×), softer spectrum, less head scatter and leakage, peaked profile.',
    'FFF is well suited to small SRS/SBRT fields, where the peaked profile matters little and the higher dose rate shortens treatment.'],
  ['ther05', 'Name the main features and limitations of a multileaf collimator.',
    'Leaf widths at isocenter of about 5 mm (central) and 10 mm, or 2.5 mm for high-definition MLCs. Rounded leaf ends, tongue-and-groove design, interleaf and transmission leakage of about 1–2%.',
    'Dynamic MLC dose depends on the dosimetric leaf gap and leaf position accuracy, tested by picket-fence and similar tests.'],
  ['ther06', 'How is geometric penumbra calculated?',
    'P = s × (SSD + d − SDD) / SDD, with s the source diameter and SDD the source-to-diaphragm distance.',
    'Most important for Co-60 (source 1.5–2 cm). Physical penumbra (80%–20%) also includes scatter and electron transport.'],
  ['ther07', 'Define the ICRU volumes.',
    'GTV: visible tumor.<br>CTV: GTV plus microscopic spread.<br>ITV: CTV plus internal motion.<br>PTV: plus set-up uncertainty.<br>PRV: an organ at risk plus a margin.',
    'From ICRU 50, 62 and 83. Treated volume is enclosed by the prescription isodose; irradiated volume receives a dose considered significant.'],
  ['ther08', 'Which dose metrics does ICRU 83 recommend for IMRT?',
    'D98% (near-minimum), D50% (median), D2% (near-maximum); homogeneity index HI = (D2 − D98) / D50.',
    'Point doses at a reference point are replaced by DVH-based reporting.'],
  ['ther09', 'What dose homogeneity does ICRU 50 recommend within the PTV?',
    'Between 95% and 107% of the prescribed dose.',
    'IMRT and SRS often allow much more heterogeneity (SRS is frequently prescribed to the 50–80% isodose).'],
  ['ther10', 'Define the RTOG and Paddick conformity indices.',
    'RTOG CI = V<sub>RI</sub> / TV (prescription isodose volume over target volume).<br>Paddick CI = TV<sub>PIV</sub>² / (TV × PIV), where TV<sub>PIV</sub> is the target volume covered by the prescription isodose.',
    'Paddick CI is 1 for perfect conformity and penalizes both under-coverage and spill.'],
  ['ther11', 'What gamma criteria does AAPM TG-218 recommend for IMRT QA?',
    '3%/2 mm, global normalization, 10% low-dose threshold. Tolerance limit: ≥ 95% passing; action limit: ≥ 90%.',
    'Gamma combines dose difference and distance to agreement: γ ≤ 1 passes.'],
  ['ther12', 'Give some key TG-142 linac QA tolerances.',
    'Daily: output ±3%; lasers ±2 mm (±1 mm for SRS); ODI ±2 mm; door interlock.<br>Monthly: output ±2%; light/radiation coincidence ±2 mm.<br>Annual: output ±1% against TG-51; isocenter (mechanical and radiation) ±1 mm (from baseline).',
    'TG-142 also has imaging QA (kV, MV, CBCT) and specific tolerances for SRS/SBRT and IMRT machines.'],
  ['ther13', 'How are wedge angle and hinge angle related?',
    'Wedge angle = 90° − (hinge angle / 2).',
    'For two fields at a 90° hinge angle, use 45° wedges. Wedge angle is defined at 10 cm depth (ICRU).'],
  ['ther14', 'How do you calculate the skin gap for abutting fields?',
    'Gap = ½L₁ × (d / SSD₁) + ½L₂ × (d / SSD₂), so the fields meet at depth d.',
    'L₁ and L₂ are the field lengths at the surface. Moving the junction (feathering) during treatment spreads out any hot or cold spot.'],
  ['ther15', 'How are craniospinal fields matched without a gap?',
    'Rotate the collimator of the lateral cranial fields by θ = arctan(½L<sub>spine</sub> / SSD<sub>spine</sub>) to match the spine field’s divergence, and kick the couch by θ = arctan(½L<sub>cranial</sub> / SAD) to match the cranial fields’ divergence.',
    'Modern plans often use VMAT or protons with overlapping gradient junctions instead.'],
  ['ther16', 'Classify dose calculation algorithms by how they handle heterogeneities.',
    'Correction-based: ratio of TARs, Batho power law, equivalent TAR.<br>Model-based: convolution/superposition (collapsed cone), AAA.<br>Most accurate: Monte Carlo, and grid-based Boltzmann solvers (Acuros XB).',
    'Pencil-beam algorithms are least accurate in lung, where they ignore changes in lateral electron transport.'],
  ['ther17', 'What happens to dose in and beyond lung?',
    'Less attenuation raises the dose beyond the lung; in the lung, lateral electron range grows, widening the penumbra and lowering the central dose for small, high-energy fields (loss of lateral CPE); a re-buildup occurs at the lung–tissue interface.',
    'This is why 6 MV is usually preferred over 18 MV for lung tumors.'],
  ['ther18', 'Give the key planning rules for electron beams.',
    'Choose E so R90 (≈ E/3.2 cm) covers the target depth; Rp ≈ E/2 cm. Bolus raises surface dose and pulls the dose toward the surface; cutouts change output (especially for small fields).',
    'Electron isodoses bulge laterally at depth for higher energies, and the 90% line constricts, so add margin.'],
  ['ther19', 'How are tissue heterogeneities handled for electron beams?',
    'Coefficient of equivalent thickness (CET): lung ≈ 0.25–0.5 (depending on its density), compact bone ≈ 1.65. Effective depth = actual depth − z(1 − CET).',
    'Edges of heterogeneities cause hot and cold spots that CET can’t predict.'],
  ['ther20', 'What are the key physics features of proton therapy?',
    'Bragg peak with no exit dose; spread-out Bragg peak (SOBP) to cover a target; clinical RBE 1.1; range uncertainty margins of about 3.5% + 1 mm.',
    'Delivery: passive scattering or pencil beam scanning (PBS, which allows IMPT). RBE rises at the distal edge, so avoid ending beams in critical organs.'],
  ['ther21', 'What does AAPM TG-101 say about SBRT?',
    'High doses in 1–5 fractions with steep gradients, needing image guidance, motion management, and overall accuracy of about 1–2 mm. It gives tables of normal-tissue dose constraints.',
    'Plans are judged by conformity and fall-off metrics such as R50% (the ratio of the 50% isodose volume to the PTV) and D2cm.'],
  ['ther22', 'What is the Winston–Lutz test?',
    'Imaging a small radiopaque ball at isocenter with small fields at many gantry, collimator and couch angles, to measure how far the radiation isocenter is from the mechanical one.',
    'For SRS, the target is a deviation of no more than 1 mm.'],
  ['ther23', 'Give some commonly used QUANTEC normal-tissue limits (conventional fractionation).',
    'Spinal cord: D<sub>max</sub> 50 Gy (≈ 0.2% myelopathy)<br>Brainstem: D<sub>max</sub> < 54 Gy<br>Optic nerve/chiasm: D<sub>max</sub> < 55 Gy<br>Parotids: mean < 25 Gy (both)<br>Lung: V<sub>20</sub> ≤ 30–35%, mean ≤ 20 Gy<br>Rectum: V<sub>70</sub> < 20%',
    'Constraints depend on the fractionation, the endpoint and the clinical protocol, so always check the protocol in use.'],
  ['ther24', 'How is risk scored in AAPM TG-100 failure mode and effects analysis?',
    'RPN = O × S × D (occurrence × severity × lack of detectability), each scored 1–10.',
    'TG-100 urges risk-based QA: put QA effort where the RPN and severity are highest, not just following prescriptive checklists.'],
  ['ther25', 'Compare kV and MV image guidance.',
    'kV (planar or CBCT): good soft-tissue contrast (photoelectric) at low dose.<br>MV (EPID portal images, MV CBCT): poor contrast (Compton), but shows the actual treatment beam and suffers less from metal artifacts.',
    'Image guidance dose should be counted, especially for frequent CBCT in children.'],
  ['ther26', 'What does AAPM TG-76 say about respiratory motion?',
    'Consider motion management when tumor motion exceeds about 5 mm. Options: 4DCT with an ITV, abdominal compression, breath-hold (e.g. DIBH), gating, or real-time tracking.',
    'DIBH is also used in left-breast treatment to move the heart away from the field.'],
  ['ther27', 'What are the key physics features of total body irradiation and total skin electron therapy?',
    'TBI: extended distance, low dose rate, lung blocks or compensators, often 12 Gy in 6 fractions twice daily, uniformity within about ±10%.<br>TSET: the Stanford six-dual-field technique with degraded ~6 MeV electrons for mycosis fungoides.',
    'Both need in vivo dosimetry to verify dose.'],
  ['ther28', 'Give the key facts about Co-60 teletherapy.',
    'Average energy 1.25 MeV (1.17 and 1.33 MeV gammas); T½ 5.27 y; output drops about 1.1% per month; large source (1.5–2 cm) gives a wide penumbra; d<sub>max</sub> 0.5 cm.',
    'Gamma Knife units use about 192–201 Co-60 sources aimed at a single focus, with 4, 8 and 16 mm collimators on current models.'],
  ['ther29', 'How is physical penumbra defined?',
    'The lateral distance between the 80% and 20% isodose lines at a given depth.',
    'It widens with depth, energy (electron range) and source size, and in low-density tissue.'],
  ['ther30', 'Compare cumulative and differential DVHs.',
    'Cumulative: the volume receiving at least each dose (what is usually shown).<br>Differential: the volume receiving dose in each small dose bin.',
    'DVHs lose all spatial information: two plans with the same DVH can put hot spots in very different places.'],

  // ───── Brachytherapy ─────
  ['brac01', 'Write the TG-43 dose-rate equation.',
    'Ḋ(r,θ) = S<sub>K</sub> × Λ × [G(r,θ) / G(r₀,θ₀)] × g(r) × F(r,θ)',
    'S<sub>K</sub>: air-kerma strength; Λ: dose-rate constant; G: geometry factor; g(r): radial dose function; F: anisotropy function.'],
  ['brac02', 'What is air-kerma strength, and its unit?',
    'S<sub>K</sub> = air-kerma rate in free space at distance d, times d² (on the transverse axis, d large compared with the source). Unit: U = 1 µGy·m²/h = 1 cGy·cm²/h.',
    'It replaced apparent activity and mg-Ra-eq as the way sources are specified.'],
  ['brac03', 'What is the TG-43 reference point, and the dose-rate constant?',
    'Reference point: r₀ = 1 cm, θ₀ = 90° (on the transverse axis).<br>Λ = dose rate to water at the reference point per unit S<sub>K</sub> (cGy·h⁻¹·U⁻¹).',
    'Typical Λ: about 1.11 for Ir-192 and about 0.97 for many I-125 seeds; it varies by source model.'],
  ['brac04', 'What are the TG-43 geometry factors for point and line sources?',
    'Point source: G = 1/r²<br>Line source: G = β / (L r sin θ), where β is the angle the source subtends at the point.',
    'The geometry factor removes inverse-square falloff, so g(r) shows only attenuation and scatter.'],
  ['brac05', 'What do the radial dose function and anisotropy function describe?',
    'g(r): attenuation and scatter along the transverse axis, normalized to 1 at 1 cm.<br>F(r,θ): the change in dose with angle around the source (from self-absorption and encapsulation), normalized to 1 at 90°.',
    'For low-energy seeds, g(r) falls quickly with distance; for Ir-192, scatter build-up keeps g(r) near 1 out to several cm.'],
  ['brac06', 'Give the half-lives and average energies of the main brachytherapy sources.',
    'Ir-192: 73.8 d, ≈ 380 keV<br>I-125: 59.4 d, ≈ 28 keV<br>Pd-103: 17.0 d, ≈ 21 keV<br>Cs-131: 9.7 d, ≈ 30 keV<br>Cs-137: 30.1 y, 662 keV<br>Co-60: 5.27 y, 1.25 MeV',
    'Low-energy seeds are easy to shield (a lead apron or thin lead works) but are very sensitive to tissue composition.'],
  ['brac07', 'What is the total dose from a permanent implant?',
    'D = Ḋ₀ × 1.44 × T½ (Ḋ₀ is the initial dose rate).',
    'The dose delivered by time t is D(t) = Ḋ₀ × 1.44 T½ × (1 − e<sup>−λt</sup>): half the total by one half-life, 75% by two.'],
  ['brac08', 'What are the usual prescription doses for prostate seed monotherapy?',
    'I-125: 145 Gy · Pd-103: 125 Gy · Cs-131: 115 Gy',
    'When combined with external beam (as a boost), the seed prescriptions are lower (for example, about 110 Gy for I-125).'],
  ['brac09', 'How is a prostate seed implant evaluated?',
    'Post-implant CT (about 1 month later for I-125). Prostate: D90 (aim ≥ 100% of the prescription), V100 (aim ≥ 90%), and V150. Also rectum and urethra doses.',
    'Doing the CT too early, while the prostate is swollen, underestimates the dose.'],
  ['brac10', 'Define Points A and B in cervical brachytherapy (Manchester system).',
    'Point A: 2 cm superior to the cervical os (along the tandem) and 2 cm lateral to the tandem.<br>Point B: 3 cm lateral to Point A (5 cm from midline), representing the pelvic side wall.',
    'Point A approximates where the uterine artery crosses the ureter. Image-guided brachytherapy has largely replaced it.'],
  ['brac11', 'What does image-guided cervix brachytherapy report?',
    'Target: HR-CTV D90 (EMBRACE II aims for > 85 Gy EQD2 with external beam).<br>Organs at risk: D2cc for bladder (< 80 Gy), rectum (< 65 Gy) and sigmoid (< 70 Gy), all EQD2 with α/β = 3.',
    'Following GEC-ESTRO and ICRU 89 recommendations.'],
  ['brac12', 'Compare the classic interstitial implant systems.',
    'Manchester (Paterson–Parker): non-uniform source distribution (more on the periphery) to give dose uniform within ±10%.<br>Quimby: uniform source distribution, so the center is hotter.<br>Paris: parallel, equidistant Ir-192 wires; reference dose = 85% of the basal dose.',
    'The basal dose is the mean of the minimum doses between the sources.'],
  ['brac13', 'What are the main properties of an HDR Ir-192 source?',
    'About 370 GBq (10 Ci) when new, a source about 0.6 mm × 3.5 mm on a cable, exchanged about every 3 months (about 1.2 half-lives).',
    'Dwell times are optimized to shape the dose; the source travels through catheters or applicators connected to the afterloader.'],
  ['brac14', 'What HDR QA is done at each source exchange and daily?',
    'Source exchange: measure S<sub>K</sub> with a calibrated well chamber and compare with the vendor certificate; check source position and timer.<br>Daily: source positioning (about ±1 mm), interlocks, warning lights, emergency equipment, survey meter.',
    'The measured strength, not the vendor’s, is entered into the planning system after investigating any significant difference.'],
  ['brac15', 'What do you do if an HDR source fails to retract?',
    'Follow the posted emergency procedure: press the emergency stop, try manual retraction, enter with a survey meter, remove the applicator with the source and place it in the emergency safe, then survey the patient and room.',
    'Keep an emergency kit (long forceps, cutters, shielded container) in the room, and rehearse the procedure.'],
  ['brac16', 'How fast does dose fall off around a brachytherapy source?',
    'Mostly by inverse square: dose at 2 cm is about ¼ of that at 1 cm, adjusted by g(r).',
    'Steep falloff spares nearby tissue but makes the dose very sensitive to positioning errors.'],
  ['brac17', 'What is the usual prescription for COMS I-125 eye plaques?',
    '85 Gy to the tumor apex (or at least 5 mm depth) over about 5–7 days.',
    'From the Collaborative Ocular Melanoma Study and ABS guidance.'],
  ['brac18', 'What is electronic brachytherapy?',
    'A miniature x-ray tube (about 50 kV) placed in an applicator, with no radioactive source.',
    'Steep dose falloff, little shielding needed; used for skin, intraoperative and breast treatments.'],
];
